{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Getting Started with vastai-host-mcp",
  "description": "A guided walkthrough of the Vast.ai host/provider MCP server architecture and development workflow",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Welcome",
      "description": "# Welcome to vastai-host-mcp\n\nThis tour walks through the current TypeScript MCP server for Vast.ai provider operations.\n\n## What you will learn\n\n- What this server is designed to do\n- How startup and transport selection work\n- How config, logging, and the API client fit together\n- How tools are registered and executed with CLI fallback\n- How pricing and reliability recommendations are generated\n\nBy the end, you should have a clear mental model for extending host tools safely."
    },
    {
      "title": "Server Scope and Purpose",
      "description": "The README defines an important boundary: this server is for host/provider workflows, not renter-side operations.\n\nRead this section first to understand the expected usage and tool intent before touching implementation details.",
      "file": "README.md",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Runtime and Scripts",
      "description": "`package.json` captures the main development and execution workflows:\n\n- `pnpm dev` runs the server directly with tsx\n- `pnpm build` compiles TypeScript\n- `pnpm start` runs the built bundle\n- `pnpm inspect` launches MCP Inspector\n\nThese scripts are the fastest way to validate local changes.",
      "file": "package.json",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Application Entry Point",
      "description": "`src/index.ts` wires together all core components: config, logger, singleton client, rules, and tool registration.\n\nNotice the server instructions string that reinforces host-only scope and how transport switches between `stdio` and `httpStream` at runtime.",
      "file": "src/index.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Environment-Driven Config",
      "description": "`src/config.ts` validates and normalizes environment variables using Zod.\n\nKey behavior:\n\n- Strong validation for required values like `VAST_API_KEY`\n- Boolean and integer parsing helpers for robust config handling\n- Cached config object for consistent access across modules\n\nIf startup fails early, this file is usually the first place to inspect.",
      "file": "src/config.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Structured Logging",
      "description": "`src/logger.ts` creates a Pino logger with ISO timestamps and sensitive field redaction.\n\nWhen adding new request paths or auth-like headers, keep this redaction strategy aligned so secrets do not leak into logs.",
      "file": "src/logger.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Host Client Design",
      "description": "`src/client/VastAIHostClient.ts` is the integration layer for Vast API and CLI fallback operations.\n\nKey patterns in this file:\n\n- Singleton client instance via `getInstance`\n- Shared `makeRequest` wrapper with normalized `/api/v0` endpoints\n- Explicit not-supported operations that intentionally fail with actionable guidance\n- CLI execution wrapper used when fallback is enabled\n\nUse this class as the single source of truth for host-side API semantics.",
      "file": "src/client/VastAIHostClient.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Automation Rules",
      "description": "`src/rules/MCPRules.ts` contains host-side advisory logic that does not directly mutate Vast resources.\n\nThere are two recommendation paths:\n\n- Pricing recommendation from price, DLPerf, reliability, and utilization\n- Reliability action generation from failures, uptime, and verification state\n\nThis separation keeps operational heuristics testable and distinct from transport concerns.",
      "file": "src/rules/MCPRules.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Tool Registration Architecture",
      "description": "`src/tools/hostTools.ts` is where MCP tools are defined and connected to client methods.\n\nObserve the shared execution helpers:\n\n- `executeWithFallback` for API-first then optional CLI fallback\n- `toContentResult` for consistent MCP response envelopes\n- `buildFailureResult` for actionable troubleshooting hints\n\nWhen adding a new tool, follow this pattern to keep behavior consistent.",
      "file": "src/tools/hostTools.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Example Tool Family",
      "description": "Further down in `src/tools/hostTools.ts`, each tool has clear schema validation and an operation-specific execution path.\n\nThis area is useful as a copy pattern for new host operations such as machine controls, diagnostics, and offer management.",
      "file": "src/tools/hostTools.ts",
      "line": 260
    },
    {
      "title": "Current Source Layout",
      "description": "The `src/` directory is intentionally small and layered:\n\n- `index.ts`: server bootstrap\n- `config.ts` + `logger.ts`: platform concerns\n- `client/`: Vast API and CLI integration\n- `rules/`: advisory host logic\n- `tools/`: MCP tool definitions\n\nKeeping responsibilities separated makes it easier to expand tool coverage without creating cross-module coupling.",
      "directory": "src"
    },
    {
      "title": "Next Steps for Contributors",
      "description": "You now have the core map of this codebase.\n\n## Suggested workflow\n\n1. Run `pnpm dev` for local development.\n2. Validate behavior with `pnpm inspect` or your MCP client.\n3. Add or adjust client methods in `src/client/VastAIHostClient.ts` first.\n4. Wire new capabilities through `src/tools/hostTools.ts`.\n5. Keep README tool coverage updated when tools change.\n\n## Helpful commands\n\n```bash\npnpm install\npnpm dev\npnpm inspect\npnpm build && pnpm start\n```\n\nUse this tour as a living onboarding map and update it whenever architecture or workflows change."
    }
  ]
}
